# 29. Assumptions

1. Care to Cure will provide authorized users to manage patient and appointment information.
2. Doctors will provide consultation, procedure, and follow-up information.
3. Standardized reason categories will be approved by business stakeholders.
4. The hospital will define the final retention measurement window.
5. Emergency encounters will be separately identified.
6. Dashboard requirements will be finalized before Power BI/Tableau development.
7. The project will use synthetic data for portfolio purposes.

---

# 30. Dependencies

The successful implementation of the system depends on:

* Stakeholder approval of requirements
* Finalization of business rules
* Standardization of reason codes
* Agreement on KPI definitions
* Agreement on retention measurement period
* Availability of patient and appointment data
* Technical architecture
* User access and security design
* Power BI/Tableau reporting requirements

---

# 31. Acceptance Criteria — High-Level

The system will be considered functionally acceptable when:

1. Users can create and manage patient records.
2. Users can create and update appointments.
3. Appointment statuses are tracked.
4. Cancellations and no-shows can be recorded.
5. Cancellation/no-show reasons can be captured.
6. Appointments can be rescheduled with traceability.
7. Consultations can be linked to appointments.
8. Procedures can be recommended and tracked.
9. Procedure completion/non-completion can be recorded.
10. Follow-ups can be created and tracked.
11. Patient feedback can be captured.
12. Drop-off points can be identified.
13. Retention KPIs can be calculated.
14. Doctor and department metrics can be generated.
15. Management dashboards can display the agreed KPIs.
16. Dashboard information can be filtered by agreed dimensions.

---

# 32. Future Reporting & Analytics

The functional system will provide the data foundation for subsequent analytics work using:

* SQL
* Power BI
* Tableau

The analytics layer will answer business questions such as:

**Which department has the highest no-show rate?**

**Why are patients cancelling appointments?**

**Which procedures have the highest drop-off?**

**How many patients miss required follow-ups?**

**Does waiting-time satisfaction correlate with patient retention?**

**Which patient segments have repeated drop-off behavior?**

**Which departments require process improvement?**

---

# 33. FRD Summary

The proposed Care to Cure Patient Retention & Follow-up Management System will transform the patient journey from fragmented operational tracking into a structured, measurable process.

The core functional journey will be:

**Patient**

↓

**Appointment**

↓

**Confirmation & Reminder**

↓

**Attendance**

↓

**Consultation**

↓

**Procedure**

↓

**Follow-up**

↓

**Feedback**

↓

**Retention / Drop-off Analysis**

The system will capture the status and relevant reasons at each major stage, enabling Care to Cure to move from simply recording patient activity to understanding **patient behavior, drop-off causes, and opportunities for process improvement.**

